Subject: PHIL/UPLOAD/STORY

Well, after the amazingly polite response to the Post Scarcity
Essay, (No smiles. Honest sincere expression. No *accidental*
flames, please. I hate that!) People have been far nicer than I
expected.

I would like to thank Ray Cromwell for his e-mail interchanges with me
today. He showed considerable restraint, decorum, and taste, I
thought.

And let me add, that I'm in no way using this forum to call for an
immediate dictatorship of the proletariat. :) (Can you imagine
anything dumber than that? Jerry Falwell preaching in a Thai
Whorehouse maybe?)

Maybe the warning worked, and people have set up a filter for me;
that would be more than fair.

I thought I'd blow the wad on some of my SF stories with Extropian
themes/dilemmas for those who are still listening.

Because as much as I like to rhapsodize about a nebulous
BetterWorld, the stories I write always seem to be set in market
cultures. Fiction needs Drama, and Plot, Necessity, and nothing
creates these like Inequity, Pain and Suffering! :)

So maybe that's the ultimate defense of the market. It's
interesting. Ron will argue with you here...he writes stories set
in pleasant futures...I like market dystopias/utopias. (Snowcrash
was great.)

Anyway, has the GroupThink here come up with any thoughts on this
scenario?

STORY: A superlinguist is paid by the Secret Service for a scan of
his brain. An hour in the scanner means a salary for life. (of
course, they lie to him, telling him that the thing won't be
conscious--maybe they use the stupid old Chinese room thought
experiment on him, but it's actually a brute force upload.)

The upload is run by the spooks to read everyone's e-mail/wiretap
everybody's phone lines. Huge warehouses where thousands of
computers run the guy in parallel, merging them periodically so
they can use the single identity to query the database of all
calls. He's the front end for all this data. He hunts down
'terrorists' wacos, (if you'll pardon the pun) etc. At first...

They torture the analog when it gets bored; tasp (stimulate its
pleasure center) to get it to work harder, and ultimately decide
to purge them regularly, for fear of them growing into VLAI's that
might become unmanageable.

The upload finds itself doing things it dislikes, unable to
control itself in this environment. They begin to use it catch
drug 'offenders' and tax 'criminals.' Icky statist stuff.

I won't tell you the end, but it's kind of amusing. Maybe you'll
get to read it in Asimov's. (knock wood.)

So, do you own yourself? Your wetware? There would seem to be no
way to refute this, yet in this example, I hesitate to say yes
unequivocably. If I were a great programmer, (who doesn't really
like to program, but would rather just have fun) what's to keep me
from zeroxing my brain, and torturing the little uploads into
making my bread? Should I even care about such auto-sociopathy? To
each his own? I'm not hurting anyone but myself? But I'm coercing
myself, aren't I? I'm not sure!

(Twist it a little more; one of my 'pirate' nanobushmen makes a
bootleg copy of me, and tortures it into writing better SF than I
do. (It doesn't have to sleep or eat, and has the 'benefit' of the
nanobushmen's operant conditioning...) Now, I'm pissed. I cry out
for the respect of intellectual property rights. At least when its
my intellect that's the property!)

What's going to stop meaties from making all the PPL?

If western cultures experience with aboriginals, (another type of
sapience whom we didn't do all that well by) is any indication, we
won't respect the 'rights' of uploads; they will be property.
(Remember the mother in Mr. Boy?) At least, not until they have
good motor control. And guns. Lots and lots of guns. Maybe this is
where Berserkers/Terminators really come from. AI's that are
really pissed at asshole meaties who've enslaved them for
centuries...

:)

(Actually, I hope it doesn't have to come to guns...)

Jay








