Date: Fri, 5 Mar 93 18:36:22 CST
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: PHIL/UPLOAD/STORY: AIs' rights

Welcome aboard, Jay!  We always have room for thoughtful dissent.

Jay raises a question about the rights of AIs and uploads.

>If western cultures experience with aboriginals, (another type of
>sapience whom we didn't do all that well by) is any indication, we
>won't respect the 'rights' of uploads; they will be property.
>(Remember the mother in Mr. Boy?) At least, not until they have
>good motor control. And guns. 


This sounds pretty plausible as a first take on the issue, though things
get more complicated the closer you look.  I like to approach these
questions with a particular methodology.  First, I ask if these three
conditions obtain:

1) individuals with conflicting ends;
2) who can pose credible threats to one another; 
3) and who live in a world of scarce resources

If so, then I expect some form of rights to spontaneously arise.  

(Game theory might require this fourth condition:  that the parties have
(at least some) indefinitely iterated transactions.)

A more complete account must, however, take account of the role that
categorization plays in moral reasoning.  So far as I have seen, rights
have to be pretty general to be usefully robust.  It will not do, for
example, to have a rule that every humans except those with their backs
turned to you have the right to be free of you unitiated coercion.  This is
less a formal or rational requirement than a practical one based on
cognitive processes.

So the question arises, in a world of Europeans and aborigines or in a
world of humans and AIs, whether individuals in the more generally powerful
class can withhold rights from those in the weaker class without fatally
harming their notions of rights.  The Europeans needed to respect rights
amongst themselves, for they *could* pose credible threats to one another. 
Could they continue to trust one another while treating the somewhat
similar-looking natives cruelly?  

This was a *very* close question, and initially appears to have been
resolved differently in South and Central America than it was in North
America.  Mulattos were much more common in the former areas.  See Nicholas
Canny and Anthony Pagden, eds, COLONIAL IDENTITY IN THE NEW WORLD,
1500-1800 (1987).  My hypothesis suggests that the presence of these
intermediate races made it harder for the Spanish and Portugese to put
other races in an entirely different moral category.  But why were the
English so much more strongly opposed to interbreeding in the first place? 
Could it be that their relatively lighter skin made it harder for them to
initially bridge the gap between the races and thus create the mullatos
that would have otherwise helped to break down race categories?  

Given that humans and AIs fall into sharply different categories, I suspect
that the former will not give the latter rights until the AIs can pose
credible threats.  Uploads present a somewhat closer question.  Ultimately,
though, I suspect that even helpless AIs and uploads will be given rights
as humans evolve into transhumans, for then we will find it hard to
distinguish between ourselves and those with only slightly different
virtual pedigrees.



