Subject: BET: Living for another 50 years

Scott Meeks writes, quoting me first:

>>P.S. In many ways you're more Extropian than me--I'm not signed up for
>>cryonics and have essentially no expectation that I'll be alive in any form
>              ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
>>in 50 years.
> ^^^^^^^^^^^^
>
>Really!  How old are you now, if you don't mind me asking?  If you answer
>anything less than 35, I'm interested in making a formal bet with you that
>you do live an additional 50 years.  
>
>        Scott

Hmmhh... If I live 50 more years, I lose. But if I die first, I win. Sounds
like insurance to me. But since I'm not less than 35, I guess the bet is
moot.

I'm 41 and guess I have a 50-50 chance to live to 80 or so, and less than a
10% chance to live to 91. Perhaps if I'd said "60 years" there'd be even
less room for manouvering.

This won't be my long-promised (threatened?) "Why I Am Not a Cryonicist"
essay, and I have no interest in starting a battle over the merits of
cryonics in general or the relative merits of Alcor vs. TransTime vs. Tim's
Smoke-Cured Suspension Service. I am not a deathist (= thinks death is a
good thing, a way for Nature to balance the books, etc.), I just don't have
much faith that enough scientific, technological, medical, cultural, and
legal advances will occur for me to get past the "hump."

For our youngest members, like Sameer Parekh, it may be barely possible.

I agree, by the way, that there is little to lose by trying cryonic
suspension, and potentially a lot to gain. If I develop a terminal illness,
I expect to sign up. But until then, I see too many problems with the
current situation. Such as:

* the existing companies seem to be in a mess. Alcor seems to be torn by
battles at the Board of Directors level (or so I hear), TransTime seems
slightly flaky, etc. Once several milllionaires leave their fortunes to
cryonics orgs, things may be on a more even keel. (Meanwhile, I'm waiting
to see what happens.)

* I fully expect every corpsicle now in suspension in the U.S. to get
thawed out, and not when the time is right! By court order, by bankruptcy
of the organizations, by raids on the cryonics labs, whatever. I can't
prove this, of course, it just seems like the way "pioneers" often go.

* if I sign up, I expect it to be in 10-15 years, in a country with
securely-bribable officials and little Christian or Muslim influence on
policies and laws. Asia looks like the right kind of place.

* If I die in an accident, far from suspension facilities, I expect the
already-slim odds are reduced still further. Hence, I won't worry about
that. If I have a terminal illness, I expect to be able to travel to the
"cryonics hospice."

* My very crude, highly personal estimate of the chances of coming back are
calculated below. I split the chance into two parts: First, the chance of
coming back at all. Second, an approximate guess on how many years I'll
live once I come back. The actual estimate is of course a convolution of
these factors.

--the incredibly optimistic estimates of cryonics immortalists:

...a 10% chance of successful reanimation (this varies from 1% to much
higher estimates, especially as some say "Just wait long enough.")
...an expected 500-year new lifetime (again, estimates range up to
thousands of years, and even higher, with uploading, additional
suspensions, cloning, etc.)
...giving "expected additional life" = 0.10 x 500 years = 50 years

--perhaps more realistic estimates:

...1% chance of successful reanimation
...100 years of additional life
...giving 1 additional year of life

--pessimistic estimates:

...0.1% estimate of successful reanimation
...100 years of additional life
...giving about a month of additional life

(You may all quibble with these estimates. Perhaps you are right. In
particular, some have disagreed with my concept of "expected additional
life," saying, basically, "Yeah, but suppose you're in that 1% group that
does get reanimated?" I agree there's room for differing interpretations.)

* Given that I hold to the less-optimistic estimates, I don't want to get
"wrapped up" in cryonics politics, in delaying choices in _this_ life
because I somehow expect to have _another_ life, and so forth. 

(This point about cryonics affecting the quality of one's life in this
pre-suspension life is deserving of its own complete essay. Certainly I
know many cryonicists who live life fully. But a real danger, at least from
our experiences with believers in "the afterlife," is that belief in
reanimation will affect choices made in _this_ life.)

* Treading on thin ice (so to speak), it may not really "matter" (whatever
that means, and here I freely admit to confusion) whether the frozen
corpsicle that my body becomes is ever reanimated. (I'm aware of how the
same argument applies to those who are asleep, or in a coma, suggesting
that we should never care if we awake or not. I have no satisfactory answer
to this one.)

That's all I'll say for now.

-Tim May, still at 37 degrees



